The thermal side of power reliability	the-thermal-side-of-power-reliability	As key technologies such as AI continue to grow in importance, datacentres are expected to work harder, faster and to be able to provide more computational power, writes Mark Masera.\nWhen developing such solutions engineers can fall into the trap of treating thermal management, power and efficiency as separate, independent problems, often assuming that solving each in isolation will produce a correct overall design. However, datacentres are more than just the sum of their parts, with thermal management, power and efficiency all being extremely interlinked.\nImportantly, redundancy in power (for example additional uninterruptible power supply capacity or backup generators) does not automatically resolve thermal challenges, as extra power capacity or uneven distribution can increase heat density, change airflow patterns and raise cooling requirements.\nThe growing datacentre problem\nThe pace of technological change over the past few years has been exponential. New hardware platforms, increasingly sophisticated algorithms and shifts in user demand have all contributed to this growth, but of all applications, AI (and in particular large language models and visual processing systems) has emerged as the singular biggest driver.\nHowever, unlike traditional cloud workloads that can serve millions of users with relatively modest hardware, AI computation is massively parallel, requiring specialised server setups including high-performance central processing units (CPUs), graphics processing units (GPUs) and access to large quantities of RAM. Furthermore, training models often spans hundreds of servers, with each rack pushing power densities that were unimaginable a few years ago.\nIn fact, the rapid pace of AI development and hardware deployment has become so severe that the industry is now facing major hard disk drive and RAM shortages, with manufacturers now prioritising server-grade components to meet AI demands. But, even where AI-rated hardware is readily available, power consumption of these systems is quickly becoming the primary bottleneck.\nTypical server racks that once consumed around 15kW now regularly draw 80kW, with next-generation designs projected to well exceed 24kW. At these levels even a few seconds of lost training time can cost hundreds of thousands of dollars in energy and time.\nRedundancy and thermal: a combined problem\nIn traditional server design, redundancy and thermal management were treated as separate considerations. When addressing reliability, one major choice that engineers had to make was the selection between hot and cold redundancy configurations. In a hot redundancy setup all power supply units (PSUs) remain powered and share the load, while in a cold redundancy setup spare units remain offline until a primary unit fails. Each approach carries different implications for thermal load, failover speed and operating cost, as summarised in Table 1.\n| Characteristic | Hot redundancy | Cold redundancy | \n| Operating state | All PSUs powered and sharing load | Spares powered off until needed | \n| Failover | Instantaneous, no interruption | Brief delay during activation | \n| Thermal load | Higher, continuous from all units | Lower, only active units dissipate heat | \n| Idle power draw | Higher | Lower | \n| Switchover reliability | High, units verified under load | Lower, depends on cold-start success | \n| Best fit | Mission-critical systems | Thermally or cost-constrained deployments | \nTable 1: Comparison of hot and cold redundancy strategies for datacentre power supplies\nAddressing thermal design, however, focused on airflow, backpressure and fan orientation. For example, misaligned fans or excessive airflow can create negative pressure zones, pulling hot air back into servers and reducing cooling efficiency. The use of a central hot channel between servers was key to preventing hot air recirculating and so long as PSU and server fans were blowing in the right direction these hot channels would remain separate from the cold channels.\nNow, in traditional deployments where processing needs were modest, these issues were very easily managed. But the introduction of AI workloads completely changed the equation. Because of the significantly higher thermal loads presented by AI server racks the inclusion of additional PSUs for reliability and redundancy is no longer straightforward.\nThe significantly higher power draw from modern AI applications (where server power can now reach 240kW) means that power supplies have to be physically larger and/or require multiple power supplies in parallel. However, the inclusion of additional PSUs can disrupt airflow, which in turn can see component temperatures rise well beyond their safe limits.\nFurthermore, the increased use of power supplies sees a much larger amount of thermal load added to the hot channel of a datacentre, which, if too much positive pressure is generated, can see hot air leaking back into the cold side and heating system components.\nThus, PSU selection now has to be done in line with the thermal considerations of the entire system.\nManaging redundancy and thermal together\nSimply reading a PSU datasheet for its basic thermal characteristics is now insufficient, where engineers now need to consider how redundancy interacts with heat removal.\nThe first step for engineers to take is to move to high-efficiency PSUs. Units rated 80 Plus Titanium dissipate less heat per watt, improving component reliability and reducing thermal stress.\nAnother option is to explore load-sharing PSUs. In these setups multiple units work in parallel to meet demand collectively, rather than relying on a single unit. If one PSU fails, the remaining units cover the load, minimising downtime. This method has the added advantage of spreading thermal load, reducing thermal hotspots in any singular PSU.\nEngineers designing modern AI server systems also need to factor in rack design and airflow management. This means that PSU fans must work with chassis airflow, not against it, so that factors such as backpressure and resistance can be minimised. Arguably one of the best methods to achieving this is to build thermal models via P-Q characteristic data or run FloTHERM simulations before deployment.\nThermal loads can be further reduced by using cold redundancy configurations when possible to prevent airflow disruption, but this comes with the trade-off of intermittent power delays during switchover.\nSystem-level co-ordination is also an essential step that engineers must now take. PSU manufacturers, chassis designers and datacentre engineers need to collaborate to optimise airflow and thermal performance (often through matching power supplies to specific rack layouts and cabinet enclosures being certified for PSUs and racks). For example, it is easy for PSU manufacturers to design their systems with one unit in mind, but if engineers require 20 stacked side by side, this is something that manufacturers need to understand.\nGoing further into the future, liquid cooling may become standard for high-density AI racks, helping manage heat from CPUs, GPUs and multiple PSUs in confined spaces. But until these systems become mainstream, air-assisted cooling will be the primary technology used in datacentres across the world.\nA balancing act\nRedundancy alone does not guarantee reliability. The addition of more PSUs increases thermal load, disrupts airflow and complicates thermal management. In high-density AI datacentres thermal constraints often determine operational limits and, by extension, the profitability of that system.\nWhen choosing PSUs for AI applications, thermal and reliability considerations are now mandatory at the system-level. Engineers must balance PSU efficiency, fan orientation, airflow paths and redundancy strategy as a single, integrated problem, not separate concerns. Smart redundancy combines hot and cold redundancy strategies with load-sharing, reducing downtime without overheating the system.\nFinally, future-proofing datacentres, now quickly becoming a key part of system design, require a thermal-aware approach, where early modelling, high-efficiency PSUs, co-ordinated airflow and advanced cooling techniques all contribute to success. When power and thermal strategies are treated as one, engineers can finally deliver reliable, high-density AI deployments that meet tomorrow’s performance demands.\nMark Masera is director of engineering for Board Mount Power at Bel.\nSee also: 1Tb and 512Gb UFS NAND samples available	[Electronics Weekly] The thermal side of power reliability As key technologies such as AI continue to grow in importance, datacentres are expected to work harder, faster and to be able to provide more computational power, writes Mark Masera. When developing such solutions engineers can fall into ...	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 17:26:13.705193+05:30	\N	\N	117	283	2026-08-13 17:26:40.966477+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.123879+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/the-thermal-side-of-power-reliability-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] The thermal side of power reliability As key technologies such as AI continue to grow in importance, datacentres are expected to work harder, faster and to be able to provide more computational power, writes Mark Masera. When developing such solutions engineers can fall into ...	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] The thermal side of power reliability As key technologies such as AI continue to grow in importance, datacentres are expected to work harder, faster and to be able to provide more computational power, writes Mark Masera When developing such solutions engineers can fall into the trap of treating thermal management, power and efficiency as separate, independent problems, often assuming that solving each in isolation will produce a correct overall design However, datacentres are more than just the sum of their parts, with thermal management, power and efficiency all being extremely interlinked.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nImportantly, redundancy in power (for example additional uninterruptible power supply capacity or backup generators) does not automatically resolve thermal challenges, as extra power capacity or uneven distribution can increase heat density, change airflow patterns and raise cooling requirements The growing datacentre problem The pace of technological change over the past few years has been exponential New hardware platforms, increasingly sophisticated algorithms and shifts in user demand have all contributed to this growth, but of all applications, AI (and in particular large language models and visual processing systems) has emerged as the singular biggest driver.	["• When developing such solutions engineers can fall into the trap of treating thermal management, power and efficiency as separate, independent problems, often assuming that solving each in isolation will produce a correct overall design", "• However, datacentres are more than just the sum of their parts, with thermal management, power and efficiency all being extremely interlinked", "• Importantly, redundancy in power (for example additional uninterruptible power supply capacity or backup generators) does not automatically resolve thermal challenges, as extra power capacity or uneven distribution can increase heat density, change airflow patterns and raise cooling requirements", "• The growing datacentre problem The pace of technological change over the past few years has been exponential", "• New hardware platforms, increasingly sophisticated algorithms and shifts in user demand have all contributed to this growth, but of all applications, AI (and in particular large language models and visual processing systems) has emerged as the singular biggest driver"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in PCB ELECTRONICS directly imp