ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects	arm-institute-calls-for-defense-manufacturing-technology-projects	Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U.S. geopolitical security. The Advanced Robotics for Manufacturing, or ARM, Institute yesterday issued a project call seeking technologies to address defense needs in metalwork, drones, munitions, shipbuilding, and more.\nProject Call 27-01 requests submissions for projects that demonstrate strong potential impact to the U.S. Department of War (DoW). They should also include a validated transition plan to the DoW’s commercial industrial base (CIB) and/or organic industrial base (OIB) and related supply chains.\nThe ARM Institute noted that it prefers dual-use technologies, which could benefit the broader U.S. manufacturing base in addition to military applications.\n“Technologies with strong potential for successful commercialization are highly desired, as this reduces DoW cost and acquisition barriers,” said the institute. “Validated transition plans will preferably be demonstrated by inclusion of DoW and/or CIB government program stakeholders on the project team.”\nU.S. military seeks faster production\nAll proposals must have a defense focus, fall between Technology Readiness Levels (TRL) 4 and 7, and include a final demonstration in a production-relevant or representative environment, said the ARM Institute. It also encouraged the inclusion of government program office or program-of-record stakeholder in submissions where applicable.\nThe ARM Institute and DoW specified that each proposal must identify and respond to one of the following defense application areas:\n- High-mix, low-volume castings and forgings\n- Submarines and high-priority surface naval vessels\n- Uncrewed aerial systems (UAS) and collaborative combat aircraft (CCA)\n- Munitions, missiles, and hypersonic weapons\n- Rapid and agile expansion of defense production\n- Agile production of rapidly evolving system designs\n- Other programs of record or validated high-priority DoW needs\nThe Pittsburgh-based institute also listed its technical topic areas relevant to this project call. It noted that submissions can focus on one or more of these technology development objectives:\n- Multi-modal Inputs for AI robotics in manufacturing\n- Rapid re-tasking and robot agility\n- Multi-robot, multi-human collaboration and supervised autonomy\n- Adaptive real-time path planning and control\n- Mobile robotics\nARM Institute provides project timeline\nWhile the project call is open to the public, concept papers must be submitted through the ARM Member Community by Sept. 16, 2026. The institute said it helps its more than 500 members to connect, collaborate, and contribute to the future of manufacturing. It will host an informational webinar on Aug. 19.\nThe ARM Institute will send invitations for the presentation phase around Oct. 13, and submissions for presentations and supporting documents will be on Dec. 2. The presentations themselves will be on or about Dec. 9, and final selections will be released on Dec. 11.\nThe subaward execution must be before Feb. 1, 2027, which is when awarded target projects will begin. The project team subcontract execution deadline is March 15, while the project period of performance end date is Jan. 31, 2028.\nThe ARM Institute said its intention is to provide a “platform for collaboration and innovation, fostering a community that works together to lift solutions off the ground that may have otherwise suffered from a lack of funding or engineering roadblocks.”\nThe ARM Institute brings together industry, academia, and government to make robotics and AI more accessible to U.S. manufacturers of all sizes, empower the workforce, strengthen global competitiveness, and elevate national resilience. It is a Manufacturing Innovation Institute (MII) funded by the Office of the Secretary of War under Agreement No. W911NF-17-3-0004 and is part of the Manufacturing USA network.	[Robotics Business Review] ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U. geopolitical security.	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 18:26:31.693789+05:30	\N	\N	116	288	2026-08-13 18:26:41.250726+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.126933+05:30	\N	1	https://www.therobotreport.com/arm-institute-calls-for-defense-manufacturing-technology-project/	Robotics Business Review	[Robotics Business Review] ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U. geopolitical security.	### Event Overview\n[Robotics Business Review] ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U geopolitical security The Advanced Robotics for Manufacturing, or ARM, Institute yesterday issued a project call seeking technologies to address defense needs in metalwork, drones, munitions, shipbuilding, and more.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nProject Call 27-01 requests submissions for projects that demonstrate strong potential impact to the U Department of War (DoW) They should also include a validated transition plan to the DoW’s commercial industrial base (CIB) and/or organic industrial base (OIB) and related supply chains.	["• geopolitical security", "• The Advanced Robotics for Manufacturing, or ARM, Institute yesterday issued a project call seeking technologies to address defense needs in metalwork, drones, munitions, shipbuilding, and more", "• Project Call 27-01 requests submissions for projects that demonstrate strong potential impact to the U", "• Department of War (DoW)", "• They should also include a validated transition plan to the DoW’s commercial industrial base (CIB) and/or organic industrial base (OIB) and related supply chains"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in ROBOTICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking industrial robots updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in Industrial Robots concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and industry best practices.	robotics	Industrial Robots	["Manufacturing"]	22	https://www.therobotreport.com/arm-institute-calls-for-defense-manufacturing-technology-project/	Robotics Business Review	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1485827404703-89b55fcc595e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80	\N	2026-08-13 18:26:41.250346+05:30	2026-08-13 18:26:41.25035+05:30	510ef68d9b8c06d4580fc553b25dc0e982b85723f4e46ff812fba1bc2a3f3b17	\N	processed	1	single_source	[Robotics Business Review] ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U. geopolitical security.	[{"heading": "Event Overview", "paragraphs": ["[Robotics Business Review] ARM Institute calls for defense manufacturing technology projects Robotics is widely seen as key to both economic competitiveness and U geopolitical security The Advanced Robotics for Manufacturing, or ARM, Institute yesterday issued a project call seeking technologies to address defense needs in metalwork, drones, munitions, shipbuilding, and more."]}, {"heading": "Context & Technical Summary", "paragraphs": ["Project Call 27-01 requests submissions for projects that demonstrate strong potential impact to the U Department of War (DoW) They should also include a validated transition plan to the DoW’s commercial industrial base (CIB) and/or organic industrial base (OIB) and related supply chains."]}]	full	f	\N
Dual Spatial-Temporal Attribution: Architecture-Aligned Post-Hoc Explainability for Recurrent Graph Anomaly Detection	dual-spatial-temporal-attribution-architecture-aligned-post-hoc-explainability-for-recurrent-graph-anomaly-detection	Computer Science > Machine Learning\n  [Submitted on 12 Aug 2026]\n    Title:Dual Spatial-Temporal Attribution: Architecture-Aligned Post-Hoc Explainability for Recurrent Graph Anomaly Detection\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n            Abstract:Deep learning detectors for anomalies in dynamic graphs have reached strong accuracy, yet they remain opaque: when an edge is flagged, the analyst receives a score but no reason. This opacity is untenable in the cooperative, regulated information systems where such detectors are deployed, where automated decisions must be auditable and trustworthy. We address this gap for AddGraph, the foundational GCN+GRU framework for edge-level anomaly detection in dynamic graphs, which to our knowledge has never been equipped with any form of explainability. We present a strictly post-hoc explainability framework, X-AddGraph, built on a Dual Spatial-Temporal Attribution (DSTA) mechanism whose three components are each aligned with one of AddGraph's architectural modules: a gradient-based relevance attribution over the current adjacency structure (spatial), a direct reading of the contextual attention weights already computed during inference (short-term temporal, at zero additional cost), and a gradient rollback through the recurrent hidden states (long-term temporal). Because the detector is frozen, detection performance is preserved exactly (Delta AUC = 0, verified empirically to ten decimal places). On the UCI Message benchmark, our trained AddGraph baseline reaches an average per-snapshot AUC of 0.8705, exceeding the originally published result; X-AddGraph reproduces every score identically while adding explanations where none existed. Evaluated across four edge populations - confident true positives, low-confidence true positives, false positives, and random samples - the long-term attribution identifies historical snapshots carrying significantly more counterfactual signal than random selection (0.127 vs. 0.074), a capability that no spatially-blind explainer can provide. We release our implementation for full reproducibility.\n    \nReferences & Citations\n    \n    Loading...\nBibliographic and Citation Tools\n            Bibliographic Explorer (What is the Explorer?)\n          \n        \n            Connected Papers (What is Connected Papers?)\n          \n        \n            Litmaps (What is Litmaps?)\n          \n        \n            scite Smart Citations (What are Smart Citations?)\n          \n        Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\n            alphaXiv (What is alphaXiv?)\n          \n        \n            CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (What is CatalyzeX?)\n          \n        \n            DagsHub (What is DagsHub?)\n          \n        \n            Gotit.pub (What is GotitPub?)\n          \n        \n            Hugging Face (What is Huggingface?)\n          \n        \n            ScienceCast (What is ScienceCast?)\n          \n        Demos\nRecommenders and Search Tools\n              Influence Flower (What are Influence Flowers?)\n            \n          \n              CORE Recommender (What is CORE?)\n            \n          \n              IArxiv Recommender\n              (What is IArxiv?)\n            \n          arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\narXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nBoth individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nHave an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? Learn more about a