$20bn is nice to have, but . . . . . . .	20bn-is-nice-to-have-but	$20 billion is nice to have, but it’s not much when you’re trying to catch the market leader in your industry and he is spending three times that amount every year.\nIf you’re catching up it’s axiomatic that you have to spend more than the person you’re trying try to catch but, in this case, Intel is spending a third as much on annual capex as TSMC is spending – $20 billion compared to $60 billion.\nDoes this mean Intel will never catch TSMC in process technology? Well, probably yes because the industry’s foundry money goes mostly to the leader in process technology, so the leader can always afford to outspend the wannabe.\nThere’s a but which lies in the tech. If you can get ahead in the tech then the big money will flow you and not your rival.\nIntel made an effort to get ahead – it scheduled the production of GAA transistors, BSPD wafers and high-NA EUV for a couple of process generations ahead of TSMC and, if the fab using those processes had yielded, Intel would have pulled off the coup of all time. But the process didn’t yield.\nWithout a breakthrough in process, the only way Intel can afford to stay in the process race is by funding its process technology development from its product divisions.\nIntel could do that by out-innovating Nvidia and/or the memory companies – assuming the datacentre chip boom lasts – but raising $60 billion or more to spend on production every year for the foreseeable future, looks like an impossible ask,	[Electronics Weekly] $20bn is nice to have, but. $20 billion is nice to have, but it’s not much when you’re trying to catch the market leader in your industry and he is spending three times that amount every year.	PUBLISHED	2026-08-14 05:56:13.078967+05:30	\N	\N	117	344	2026-08-14 05:56:41.352602+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.168617+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/blogs/mannerisms/dilemmas/20bn-is-nice-but-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] $20