ASSBs moving to pilot production	assbs-moving-to-pilot-production	ASSB Technology Readiness Levels (TRLs) have advanced significantly in 2026, reports TrendForce, with Toyota, Honda, Nissan, and Samsung among the companies leading the way with pilot line production.\nJapanese suppliers have already verified key automotive-grade performance metrics and are speeding up the assessment of ASSB modules for vehicle use.\nAcross key development regions including China, Japan, and South Korea, ASSB performance validation has progressed beyond the laboratory proof-of-concept stage (TRL 1–3), with some leading companies advancing toward TRL 5–6.\nThis marks a shift in R&D priorities from achieving laboratory-scale material breakthroughs to improving manufacturing processes, ensuring product consistency, and meeting automotive qualification requirements.\nLeading Japanese and Korean companies achieved breakthroughs in ASSB sample validation on pilot engineering lines during the first half of 2026, with automotive ASSB cells completing partial performance and reliability verification.\nThe cells met design targets for key metrics, including charge-discharge performance and durability.\nFor example, Nissan’s thick-format 23-layer stacked automotive ASSB cell has completed charge-discharge testing and demonstrated better capacity retention than conventional liquid lithium-ion batteries.\nMeanwhile, leading Chinese companies including CATL, BYD, and FAW continued advancing small-scale ASSB pilot lines, accelerating progress toward TRL 4–5.\nAlongside automotive-grade validation, ASSB applications are expanding from EVs into higher-value markets.\nTrendForce uncovered that SSB products from companies including Samsung SDI, Maxell, Ilika, ProLogium, and Factorial are targeting drones/eVTOLs, microelectronics, construction machinery, and embodied robots, with several applications and orders beginning to materialise.	[Electronics Weekly] ASSBs moving to pilot production ASSB Technology Readiness Levels (TRLs) have advanced significantly in 2026, reports TrendForce, with Toyota, Honda, Nissan, and Samsung among the companies leading the way with pilot line production. Japanese suppliers have already verified k...	PUBLISHED	2026-08-14 11:01:20.478932+05:30	\N	\N	116	369	2026-08-14 11:02:23.752823+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.18752+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/solid-state-auto-batteries-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] ASSBs moving to pilot production ASSB Technology Readiness Levels (TRLs) have advanced significantly in 2026, reports TrendForce, with Toyota, Honda, Nissan, and Samsung among the companies leading the way with pilot line production. Japanese suppliers have already verified k...	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] ASSBs moving to pilot production ASSB Technology Readiness Levels (TRLs) have advanced significantly in 2026, reports TrendForce, with Toyota, Honda, Nissan, and Samsung among the companies leading the way with pilot line production Japanese suppliers have already verified key automotive-grade performance metrics and are speeding up the assessment of ASSB modules for vehicle use Across key development regions including China, Japan, and South Korea, ASSB performance validation has progressed beyond the laboratory proof-of-concept stage (TRL 1–3), with some leading companies advancing toward TRL 5–6.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nThis marks a shift in R&D priorities from achieving laboratory-scale material breakthroughs to improving manufacturing processes, ensuring product consistency, and meeting automotive qualification requirements Leading Japanese and Korean companies achieved breakthroughs in ASSB sample validation on pilot engineering lines during the first half of 2026, with automotive ASSB cells completing partial performance and reliability verification The cells met design targets for key metrics, including charge-discharge performance and durability.	["• Japan