Obsolescence planning on long life-cycle programmes	obsolescence-planning-on-long-life-cycle-programmes	ATGBICS, one of Astute Group’s franchises, has brought out a white paper, “Strategic Approaches to Obsolescence Management in Network Connectivity,” which looks at how OEMs and integrators in defence, aerospace and industrial markets can plan for component end-of-life risk on long-lifecycle programmes.\nObsolescence remains one of the most persistent cost and schedule risks on multi-decade defence and aerospace platforms, where a connector or cable assembly can outlive its original semiconductor or passive component supply chain many times over. ATGBICS’s paper sets out a structured approach to anticipating and managing this risk across the network connectivity layer specifically — an area that often receives less proactive lifecycle planning than the active electronics it supports.\nFor engineers and programme managers, the paper is a useful reference point for building — or auditing — an obsolescence management plan, particularly for platforms already committed to a specific connector or cabling architecture with limited redesign headroom. For procurement and supply chain teams, it reinforces the case for early engagement with franchised distribution partners who can flag end-of-life notices and qualify alternates before a shortage becomes a production-stopping event.\nAstute, as a franchised ATGBICS partner, can support customers applying this framework to their own bills of materials — identifying at-risk connectivity parts and sourcing qualified alternates ahead of end-of-life.\nNext step: request the full white paper via Astute or directly from ATGBICS to review the framework against your own platform’s connectivity BOM.	[Electronics Weekly] Obsolescence planning on long life-cycle programmes ATGBICS, one of Astute Group’s franchises, has brought out a white paper, “Strategic Approaches to Obsolescence Management in Network Connectivity,” which looks at how 