The Best We Can Hope For Is A Soft Landing	the-best-we-can-hope-for-is-a-soft-landing	The unprecedented 102.5% increase in the H1 semi market comes with a warning from Future Horizons’ CEO Malcolm Penn (pictured).\n“Growth is being driven by price (up 79.8 percent) and not units (up only 12.1 percent),” says Penn, “and it is not broadly based but driven by a single end market application, the white-hot AI hyperscaler boom and its deep-pocketed, at least for now, customers, across a limited number of product sectors.”\nIn the industry’s 79-year history there has never been such a growth rate. June 2026 was the 34th consecutive month of positive year-over-year growth making it the second longest growth period on record, with only the 35-month June 2002-May 2005 upturn longer, but only by one month.\n“What we are seeing is a chip market that is currently dominated by AI, with its associated product shortages and sky-high ASPs,” says Penn, “the much broader, traditional chip markets are growing much more modestly, especially the consumer-drive automotive, computer and smartphone sectors, weighed down by an overall still relatively weak global economy and inflated memory costs.”\n“We strongly believe any ASP-driven growth is unsustainable in the long-term and a downward correction in pricing is inevitable, either because new suppliers or alternative lower-cost solutions enter the market, or because the current product shortages are alleviated, either because additional capacity comes on stream or the end market demand softens,” says Penn.\n‘The best one can hope for is a soft-landing adjustment, but this seems unlikely given the extreme polarisation of the current market growth,” says Penn, “there simply is no other sector able to afford the high IC and associated advanced packaging costs or pick up the volume slack were this build out to slow down or worse still collapse.”	[Electronics Weekly] The Best We Can Hope For Is A Soft Landing The unprecedented 102. 5% increase in the H1 semi market comes with a warning from Future Horizons’ CEO Malcolm Penn (pictured).	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 12:29:27.422243+05:30	\N	\N	116	243	2026-08-13 12:30:13.640851+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.095729+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/the-best-we-can-hope-for-is-a-soft-landing-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] The Best We Can Hope For Is A Soft Landing The unprecedented 102. 5% increase in the H1 semi market comes with a warning from Future Horizons’ CEO Malcolm Penn (pictured).	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] The Best We Can Hope For Is A Soft Landing The unprecedented 102 5% increase in the H1 semi market comes with a warning from Future Horizons’ CEO Malcolm Penn (pictured) “Growth is being driven by price (up 79.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\n8 percent) and not units (up only 12 1 percent),” says Penn, “and it is not broadly based but driven by a single end market application, the white-hot AI hyperscaler boom and its deep-pocketed, at least for now, customers, across a limited number of product sectors ” In the industry’s 79-year history there has never been such a growth rate.	["• 5% increase in the H1 semi market comes with a warning from Future Horizons’ CEO Malcolm Penn (pictured)", "• “Growth is being driven by price (up 79", "• 8 percent) and not units (up only 12", "• 1 percent),” says Penn, “and it is not broadly based but driven by a single end market application, the white-hot AI hyperscaler boom and its deep-pocketed, at least for now, customers, across a limited number of product sectors", "• ” In the industry’s 79-year history there has never been such a growth rate"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in PCB ELECTRONICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical ground