British car beats world land speed record at 406 mph	british-car-beats-world-land-speed-record-at-406-mph	The hydrogen-powered JCB Hydromaxchas set a new world land speed record of 406.32 mph.\nThe car recorded the average speed 400.623 mph in its first run across the Bonneville Salt Flats, and 412.135 mph on its second run, giving the average of 406.320 mph – the fastest a hydrogen internal combustion car has ever travelled.\nDriven by Wing Commander Andy Green OBE, the twin engined, 32-foot car completed the two runs required under FIA rules – one in each direction within an hour – comfortably beating the previous FIA-officiated hydrogen internal combustion benchmark of 185.5 mph set by the BMW H2R in 2004.\nThe record is subject to official ratification by the Fédération Internationale de l’Automobile (FIA), the global governing body for motorsport.\nIn doing so, JCB Hydromax surpassed the 350.092 mph world diesel land speed record set by JCB Dieselmax — also driven by Green, on the same salt, in August 2006.\nTwenty years on, JCB has beaten its own benchmark with an engine that produces no CO₂ at the tailpipe.\nThe speed also exceeds the 303 mph hydrogen fuel cell mark — making JCB Hydromax the fastest hydrogen-powered vehicle of any kind in history.\nThe record – set in the Category A, Group XIV – Class 7 FIA World Flying Record Start Land Speed Record – was set using two of JCB’s own production-based hydrogen digger engines, producing a combined 1,600 bhp and manufactured at the company’s engine factory in Foston, Derbyshire, UK.\nThey are the same engines now powering JCB machines and equipment rolling off production lines in the UK as part of the company’s £100 million hydrogen programme.\n“Twenty years ago we came to Bonneville with JCB Dieselmax and showed what British engineering could do with diesel power,” said JCB chairman Lord Bamford, “today we have done it again — this time with engines powered by hydrogen. This record was set by production-based engines, the same engines powering JCB diggers right now. That is the point of JCB Hydromax: it shows hydrogen works, and it works today at the highest level with zero emissions.”\n“Bonneville is the spiritual home of the world land speed record, and JCB Hydromax has just written itself into that history,” said Andy Green, “the car was terrific — stable, strong and fast. Setting a world land speed record with hydrogen power, twenty years after Dieselmax, is a huge privilege. This record is a huge achievement by a world class team and superb technology.”	[Electronics Weekly] British car beats world land speed record at 406 mph The hydrogen-powered JCB Hydromaxchas set a new world land speed record of 406. The car recorded the average speed 400.	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 12:29:27.42226+05:30	\N	\N	117	244	2026-08-13 12:30:13.997423+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.096968+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/british-car-beats-world-land-speed-record-at-406-mph-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] British car beats world land speed record at 406 mph The hydrogen-powered JCB Hydromaxchas set a new world land speed record of 406. The car recorded the average speed 400.	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] British car beats world land speed record at 406 mph The hydrogen-powered JCB Hydromaxchas set a new world land speed record of 406 The car recorded the average speed 400 623 mph in its first run across the Bonneville Salt Flats, and 412.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\n135 mph on its second run, giving the average of 406 320 mph – the fastest a hydrogen internal combustion car has ever travelled Driven by Wing Commander Andy Green OBE, the twin engined, 32-foot car completed the two runs required under FIA rules – one in each direction within an hour – comfortably beating the previous FIA-officiated hydrogen internal combustion benchmark of 185.	["• The car recorded the average speed 400", "• 623 mph in its first run across the Bonneville Sal