UK Chain Hotels Fare Better Than Independents	uk-chain-hotels-fare-better-than-independents	In the UK, chain hotels consistently report more positive business sentiment than independents across all surveyed metrics, according to the 2026 UK Accommodation Barometer.\nChains score higher on economic conditions, key performance indicators, room rates and occupancy.\nThey also tend to rate their access to financing, investment capacity and pace of technology adoption more favorably. Independent hotels track more conservatively across each of these areas.\nChains are typically better positioned to absorb economic pressures and leverage scale, whether through investment capacity, pace of technology adoption, or more favourable access to financing.\nThis gap widened significantly in 2026 across key metrics like room rate trends, occupancy, and access to capital, as chain hotels providers trended up while independent hotels saw little to no increase in positive sentiment.\nDavid Manners has more than forty-years experience writing about the electronics industry, its major trends and leading players. As well as writing business, components and research news, he is the author of the site's most popular blog, Mannerisms. This features series of posts such as Fables, Markets, Shenanigans, and Memory Lanes, across a wide range of topics.\nGet our news, blogs and comments straight to your inbox! Sign up for the Electronics Weekly newsletters: Mannerisms, Gadget Master and the Daily and Weekly roundups.\nThe excellent Electronics Weekly podcast, CHIIPS, is brought to you by our editor Caroline Hayes. Hear interesting insights from a range of industry figures. Catch all the episodes »\nRead our special features celebrating 65 years of Electronics Weekly and looking ahead to the future of the industry. Read the Electronics Weekly @ 65 features »\nRead the first ever Electronics Weekly online: 7th September 1960. We’ve scanned the very first edition so you can enjoy it. Read the very first edition »	In the UK, chain hotels consistently report more positive business sentiment than independents across all surveyed metrics, according to the 2026 UK Accommodation Barometer	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:03.750428+05:30	\N	\N	110	146	2026-08-13 09:35:03.754075+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.140891+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/blogs/mannerisms/consumer/uk-chain-hotels-fare-better-than-independents-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] UK Chain Hotels Fare Better Than Independents In the UK, chain hotels consistently report more positive business sentiment than independents across all surveyed metrics, according to the 2026 UK Accommodation Barometer. Chains score higher on economic conditions, key performa...	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] UK Chain Hotels Fare Better Than Independents In the UK, chain hotels consistently report more positive business sentiment than independents across all surveyed metrics, according to the 2026 UK Accommodation Barometer Chains score higher on economic conditions, key performance indicators, room rates and occupancy They also tend to rate their access to financing, investment capacity and pace of technology adoption more favorably.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nIndependent hotels track more conservatively across each of these areas Chains are typically better positioned to absorb economic pressures and leverage scale, whether through investment capacity, pace of technology adoption, or more favourable access to financing This gap widened significantly in 2026 across key metrics like room rate trends, occupancy, and access to capital, as chain hotels providers trended up while independent hotels saw little to no increase in positive sentiment.	["• Chains score higher on economic conditions, key performance indicators, room rates and occupancy", "• They also tend to rate their access to financing, investment capacity and pace of technology adoption more favorably", "• Independent hotel