BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for	birdcalls-how-an-old-payphone-became-a-decade-long-lesson-in-what-technology-is-for	BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for\nThere’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland. Pick up the handset, press a number, and instead of a dial tone you hear a night heron, a red-tailed hawk, a wood thrush. No spare change needed. No app to download. Just a phone, a button, and the sound of a bird that might be living in the creek down the street.\nThis is BirdCalls, a project by David Schulman – sound designer, violinist, composer, audio producer, and adjunct lecturer at Georgetown University – and it’s been quietly running on Arduino hardware for almost a decade.\nArduino has always had a particular kind of relationship with artists and musicians. Ease of use and accessibility were never just selling points for makers and engineers; they opened the door for people whose first instinct is creative, not technical, to bring an idea to life without needing a degree in electronics to do it. BirdCalls is a clear example of what happens when that door is wide open.\n“An old piece of tech that reconnects us to nature”\nThe project began in 2016, when Takoma Park asked residents for ideas on what to do with a payphone that technology had made obsolete. Schulman’s pitch: hot-wire it to play birdsong. He’d long been familiar with the field recordings held by the Cornell Lab of Ornithology’s Macaulay Library, and he’d always loved payphones as objects. BirdCalls put the two together.\nAs NPR reported when they featured the project in 2024, the payphone has become a local fixture: picked up by neighborhood children, by adults waiting at the bus stop, by anyone curious enough to lift the handset. Schulman told NPR he’d love to build more BirdCalls boxes elsewhere, featuring birds specific to those places. He mentioned wanting to feature coua calls from Madagascar, sounds so unusual people might wonder what planet they’re hearing from. When we spoke to him recently, he captured the spirit of the whole project in a simple sentence: “I like the idea of an old piece of tech that reconnects us to nature.”\nThese days, STEM education and AI innovation make headlines every day – and rightly so. But BirdCalls reminds us that technology has always had another job too: giving people a way to express something, and to experience something, that words alone don’t quite cover. A payphone that plays birdsong isn’t trying to optimize anything. It’s just offering a sensory experience, and helping you notice the world around you a little more.\nPhoto provided by The Willows Community School, Culver City\nFrom a Maryland sidewalk to a Los Angeles schoolyard\nThe project’s reach has grown well beyond that one yellow box. At The Willows Community School in Culver City, California, a BirdCalls phone was installed on the outside of one of the buildings after Head of School Lisa Rosenstein heard the NPR segment and asked science teacher Ann Istrin to look into bringing one to campus. Donated by The Willows’ Class of 2024, the phone is now used by students throughout the day and features 10 birds native to the local area, from the Allen’s Hummingbird to the Great Horned Owl. Rosenstein described the phone as an “interactive, experiential, and inspirational tool” that extends the science curriculum while connecting students to the nature around them.\nThat story is about to repeat itself on the other side of the country. This fall, Wake Forest Elementary School in North Carolina will have its own BirdCalls phone installed and running – a new chapter for a project that started with one phone box and one town’s birds, and keeps finding new schoolyards and new species to feature.\nBranden Hall, hot-wiring the original BirdCall phone\nSimple by design\nThe technology behind BirdCalls is refreshingly modest. The original 2016 phone was hot-wired by programmer, designer, and engineer Branden Hall, who has continued to generously consult on code troubleshooting for Schulman’s more recent installations. In 2024, Schulman worked with programmer and engineer Ryn Slack to build new code from scratch for the next generation of BirdCalls phones, adding capabilities like randomized playback, so the experience feels a little different every time someone picks up the handset.\nAcross the various installations, the team has relied on Arduino® UNO™ and Arduino® Nano™ boards. There’s no cloud connection, no app, no Wi-Fi required – the device just needs power. All the audio files and bird information live locally on an SD card, which means each BirdCalls phone runs entirely on its own, indefinitely, with nothing to maintain beyond keeping the lights on.\nThat simplicity is the whole point. Schulman didn’t need a complex system to build something that’s meant something to thousands of people across two coasts. He needed a board reliable enough to trust, simple enough to learn quickly, and flexible enough to get out of the way of the idea. Sometimes the most interesting use of a microcontroller isn’t the most technically ambitious one. It’s the one that gets a kid at a bus stop to stop, pick up a phone that doesn’t ring, and listen to a bird they didn’t know was their neighbor.\nArduino, UNO, and Nano are trademarks or registered trademarks of Arduino S.r.l.	There’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:24.1067+05:30	\N	\N	116	197	2026-08-13 09:35:24.127567+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.271029+05:30	\N	1	https://blog.arduino.cc/2026/07/22/birdcalls-how-an-old-payphone-became-a-decade-long-lesson-in-what-technology-is-for/	Arduino Blog	[Arduino Blog] BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for There’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland. Pick up the handset, press a number, and in...	### Event Overview\n[Arduino Blog] BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for There’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland Pick up the handset, press a number, and instead of a dial tone you hear a night heron, a red-tailed hawk, a wood thrush No spare change needed.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nJust a phone, a button, and the sound of a bird that might be living in the creek down the street This is BirdCalls, a project by David Schulman – sound designer, violinist, composer, audio producer, and adjunct lecturer at Georgetown University – and it’s been quietly running on Arduino hardware for almost a decade Arduino has always had a particular kind of relationship with artists and musicians.	["• Pick up the handset, press a number, and instead of a dial tone you hear a night heron, a red-tailed hawk, a wood thrush", "• No spare change needed", "• Just a phone, a button, and the sound of a bird that might be living in the creek down the street", "• This is BirdCalls, a project by David Schulman – sound designer, violinist, composer, audio producer, and adjunct lecturer at Georgetown University – and it’s been quietly running on Arduino hardware for almost a decade", "• Arduino has always had a particular kind of relationship with artists and musicians"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in IOT directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in General concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and industry best practices.	iot	General	["IOT", "General"]	27	https://blog.arduino.cc/2026/07/22/birdcalls-how-an-old-payphone-became-a-decade-long-lesson-in-what-technology-is-for/	Arduino Blog	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558346490-a72e53ae2d4f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80	\N	2026-08-13 09:35:24.127399+05:30	2026-08-13 09:35:24.1274+05:30	0845068b17a054ee16803e309a454b4c2b5aaeec1137fa6acdc1cef4b1cf3237	\N	processed	1	single_source	[Arduino Blog] BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for There’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland. Pick up the handset, press a number, and in...	[{"heading": "Event Overview", "paragraphs": ["[Arduino Blog] BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for BirdCalls: how an old payphone became a decade-long lesson in what technology is for There’s a yellow payphone bolted to a wall in Takoma Park, Maryland Pick up the handset, press a number, and instead of a dial tone you hear a night heron, a red-tailed hawk, a wood thrush No spare change needed."]}, {"heading": "Context & Technical Summary", "paragraphs": ["Just a phone, a button, and the sound of a bird that might be living in the creek down the street This is BirdCalls, a project by David Schulman – sound designer, violinist, composer, audio producer, and adjunct lecturer at Georgetown University – and it’s been quietly running on Arduino hardware for almost a decade Arduino has always had a particular kind of relationship with artists and musicians."]}]	full	f	\N
Apple Vision Pro Owners Can Soon Watch Live Immersive Friday Night Baseball	apple-vision-pro-owners-can-soon-watch-live-immersive-friday-night-baseball	Friday Night Baseball is coming to Apple Immersive starting on August 28.\nRight off the bat, the live stream will be widely available to Apple Vision Pro users with an Apple TV subscription both in the Major League Baseball home countries of the U.S. and Canada, as well as Australia, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK.\nFans attending the games virtually will have the ability to visit Yankee Stadium, PNC Park, Oracle Park and Dodger Stadium this season, each with their own unique camera views. Ambisonic microphones will capture the sounds of the locations in Spatial Audio adding to the sense of presence at each spot, even during breaks in the action. The live experience will pair the immersive stream with a graphics package that floats in the visitor’s space and include details about the team lineups and stats. It also includes replays and play-by-play commentary from Paul Severino, analysis from Xavier Scruggs and sideline reporting from Michelle Margaux.\nFor those unable to attend live in Apple Immersive, full game replays and a highlights package can be watched later directly in the Apple TV app. Just the highlights package does not require an Apple TV subscription, so it can act as a way to encourage fans to subscribe to access full games.\nApple has an opportunity to leverage its non-immersive Friday Night Baseball streams and the marketing around them to effectively showcase what premium immersive sports broadcasts like this offer to drive more engagement with Apple Vision Pro. Given Apple currently holds Major League Baseball and Major League Soccer broadcast rights for a few more years, I would bet that there is more live sports access to come in addition to investment in more epic sports documentary content like Apple Immersive's Real Madrid: The Weight of Greatness.\nOne game will be broadcast live each week for the remainder of this “Friday Night Baseball” season. The games that will receive the Apple Immersive treatment are highlighted above with the Apple Vision Pro icon.	Live Friday Night Baseball is coming to Apple Immersive later this month, bringing MLB to Apple Vision Pro users with an Apple TV subscription	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:21.765427+05:30	\N	\N	116	181	2026-08-13 09:35:21.782204+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.282001+05:30	\N	1	https://www.uploadvr.com/apple-expands-friday-night-baseball-with-live-immersive-access-on-vision-pro/	UploadVR	[UploadVR] Apple Vision Pro Owners Can Soon Watch Live Immersive Friday Night Baseball Friday Night Baseball is coming to Apple Immersive starting on August 28. Right off the bat, the live stream will be widely available to Apple Vision Pro users with an Apple TV subscription both in the Major Le...	### Event Overview\n[UploadVR] Apple Vision Pro Owners Can Soon Watch Live Immersive Friday Night Baseball Friday Night Baseball is coming to Apple Immersive starting on August 28 Right off the bat, the live stream will be widely available to Apple Vision Pro users with an Apple TV subscription both in the Major League Baseball home countries of the U and Canada, as well as Australia, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, and the UK.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nFans attending the games virtually will have the ability to visit Yankee Stadium, PNC Park, Oracle Park and Dodger Stadium this season, each with their own unique camera views Ambisonic microphones will capture the sounds of the locations in Spatial Audio adding to the sense of presence at each spot, even during breaks in the action The live experience will pair the immersive stream with a graphics package that floats in the visitor’s space and include details about the team lineups and stats.	["• Right off the bat, the live stream will be widely available to Apple Vision Pro users with an Apple TV subscription both in the Maj