Imec proposes a mix of CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V for datacentre connectivity	imec-proposes-a-mix-of-cmos-bicmos-and-iii-v-for-datacentre-connectivity	Imec has proposed an approach to connectivity inside datacentres which integrates CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V chiplets.\nIt uses cost-effective manufacturing while operating at 100GHz+ frequencies.\nWhile Si-CMOS technology offers the cost advantages of high-volume manufacturing, its performance falls short at frequencies beyond 100 GHz.\nBy contrast, III-V materials such as InP and GaN)m deliver superior gain, output power, and efficiency – yet remain significantly more difficult and costly to manufacture at scale.\n“And for optical interconnect building blocks such as drivers and transimpedance amplifiers (TIAs), there’s even a third contender: BiCMOS – a semiconductor platform that combines the scalability of CMOS with the speed of bipolar transistors,” says Imec’s Joris van Driessche (pictured ),“BiCMOS can deliver the required bandwidth and performance without relying on III-V materials, but it still comes with the challenge of how to preserve that performance across the interconnects linking these drivers and TIAs to the system’s digital signal processors and photonic integrated circuits.”\nThe solution, explains van Driessche, is to combine strengths. “Rather than building an entire system in III-V or BiCMOS technology, you use compact III-V or BiCMOS chiplets only for the performance-critical functions where their advantages justify the additional cost. These chiplets are then heterogeneously integrated on an RF silicon interposer that hosts the remaining passives and can provide ultra-low-loss interconnects at frequencies up to 325 GHz.”\n“It’s an approach that strongly resonates with hyperscalers and the broader ecosystem,” says Van Driessche. “They recognize that III-Vs and BiCMOS deliver outstanding RF performance. So, the next question is whether the economics work. RF silicon interposer technology may not be the lowest-cost option at a component level, but as systems become more complex, it has the potential to significantly reduce overall cost, footprint, and integration complexity.”\nEarly design assessments support that potential, suggesting that imec’s RF silicon interposer technology could reduce III-V chiplet area by more than a factor of three without sacrificing functionality – while creating opportunities for enhanced RF performance.\nThis is achieved through finer-pitch (flip-chip) integration, and by offloading passive components onto the interposer.	Imec has proposed an approach to connectivity inside datacentres which integrates CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V chiplets	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:03.750559+05:30	\N	\N	117	153	2026-08-13 09:35:03.773284+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.297683+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/imec-proposes-a-mix-of-cmos-bicmos-and-iii-v-for-datacentre-connectivity-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] Imec proposes a mix of CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V for datacentre connectivity Imec has proposed an approach to connectivity inside datacentres which integrates CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V chiplets. It uses cost-effective manufacturing while operating at 100GHz+ frequencies.	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] Imec proposes a mix of CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V for datacentre connectivity Imec has proposed an approach to connectivity inside datacentres which integrates CMOS, BiCMOS and III-V chiplets It uses cost-effective manufacturing while operating at 100GHz+ frequencies While Si-CMOS technology offers the cost advantages of high-volume manufacturing, its performance falls short at frequencies beyond 100 GHz.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nBy contrast, III-V materials such as InP and GaN)m deliver superior gain, output power, and efficiency – yet remain significantly more difficult and costly to manufacture at scale “And for optical interconnect building blocks such as drivers and transimpedance amplifiers (TIAs), there’s even a third contender: BiCMOS – a semiconductor platform that combines the scalability of CMOS with the speed of bipolar transistors,” says Imec’s Joris van Driessche (pictured ),“BiCMOS can deliver the required bandwidth and performance without relying on III-V materials, but it still comes with the challenge of how to preserve that performance across the interconnects linking these drivers and TIAs to the system’s digital signal processors and photonic integrated circuits ” The solution, explains van Driessche, is to combine strengths.	["• It uses cost-effective manufacturing while operating at 100GHz+ frequencies", "• While Si-CMOS technology offers the cost advantages of high-volume manufacturing, its performance falls short at frequencies beyond 100 GHz", "• By contrast, III-V materials such as InP and GaN)m deliver superior gain, output power, and efficiency – yet remain significantly more difficult and costly to manufacture at scale", "• “And for optical interconnect building blocks such as drivers and transimpedance amplifiers (TIAs), there’s even a third contender: BiCMOS – a semiconductor platform that combines the scalability of CMOS with the speed of bipolar transistors,” says Imec’s Joris van Driessche (pictured ),“BiCMOS can deliver the required bandwidth and performance without relying on III-V materials, but it still comes with the challenge of how to preserve that performance across the interconnects linking these drivers and TIAs to the system’s digital signal processors and photonic integrated circuits", "• ” The solution, explains van Driessche, is to combine strengths"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in PCB ELECTRONICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in General concepts, real-world deployment patte