Astrolight, ATMOS Space Cargo sign MoU for in-flight laser comms	astrolight-atmos-space-cargo-sign-mou-for-in-flight-laser-comms	Astrolight and ATMOS Space Cargo have signed an MoU (memorandum of understanding) to jointly demonstrate what they describe as the first in-flight optical communications link between a re-entry spacecraft and an orbiting satellite.\nThe wider goal is to integrate laser comms into future cargo-return missions, giving operators faster access to in-flight data. This would boost Europe’s goal for scalable space return logistics, they say.\nAstrolight, a space communications startup, and ATMOS, a developer of reusable and modular return space capsules, are planning to run the test in 2027.\nThis will be to enable real-time, resilient communication during re-entry. Basically, to support the return of in-orbit manufactured products. As well as possibly critical hardware for defense, Earth observation, disaster response, and future in-space logistics.\nSpecifically, during the mission, Astrolight’s ATLAS-X laser communication terminals will fly onboard both ATMOS’ PHOENIX re-entry vehicle and the satellite used for the test to demonstrate a spacecraft-to-satellite optical link.\nThis will enable the real-time transfer of system and mission data during in-orbit operations and re-entry. The data rate will be 2.5 Gbps rate.\nRe-entry comms\n“Testing a laser link between PHOENIX and an orbiting satellite will mark an important first for re-entry communications,” said Laurynas Mačiulis, CEO of Astrolight. “Until now, this capability has only been explored in ground-based laboratory conditions. Together with ATMOS Space Cargo, we are bringing it into space.”\n“Our goal is to help re-entry vehicles connect directly with satellites and, in the future, satellite constellations, so operators can access as much data as possible in real time and make missions more controlled and scalable. Laser links also make communications harder to interfere with or intercept – a major advantage for both commercial and defence missions.”\nSovereignty\nFor its part ATMOS highlighted sovereign access to space. Sebastian Klaus, CEO of ATMOS Space Cargo, commented:\n“ATMOS is working to give Europe independent and routine commercial access to return from space,” he said .\n“As cargo-return missions grow more autonomous and data-intensive, real-time connectivity across the entire mission cycle is becoming increasingly important. Our partnership with Astrolight is a step toward integrating laser communication into PHOENIX as a strategic layer for payload monitoring, autonomous de-orbit, and re-entry operations.”\nATMOS\nWith its HQ in Vilnius, Lithuania, Astrolight specialises in space-to-Earth and ship-to-ship laser communications.\nA Rheinmünster, Germany-based company, ATMOS has been selected by the European Innovation Council (EIC) Accelerator. This is a programme of the European Union’s (EU) Horizon Europe framework.	Astrolight and ATMOS Space Cargo have signed an MoU (memorandum of understanding) to jointly demonstrate what they describe as the first in-flight optical communications link between a re-entry spacecraft and [&#8230;...	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:03.750511+05:30	\N	\N	117	150	2026-08-13 09:35:03.766131+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.194986+05:30	\N	1	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/astrolight-atmos-space-cargo-sign-mou-for-in-flight-laser-comms-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	[Electronics Weekly] Astrolight, ATMOS Space Cargo sign MoU for in-flight laser comms Astrolight and ATMOS Space Cargo have signed an MoU (memorandum of understanding) to jointly demonstrate what they describe as the first in-flight optical communications link between a re-entry spacecraft and an...	### Event Overview\n[Electronics Weekly] Astrolight, ATMOS Space Cargo sign MoU for in-flight laser comms Astrolight and ATMOS Space Cargo have signed an MoU (memorandum of understanding) to jointly demonstrate what they describe as the first in-flight optical communications link between a re-entry spacecraft and an orbiting satellite The wider goal is to integrate laser comms into future cargo-return missions, giving operators faster access to in-flight data This would boost Europe’s goal for scalable space return logistics, they say.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nAstrolight, a space communications startup, and ATMOS, a developer of reusable and modular return space capsules, are planning to run the test in 2027 This will be to enable real-time, resilient communication during re-entry Basically, to support the return of in-orbit manufactured products.	["• The wider goal is to integrate laser comms into future cargo-return missions, giving operators faster access to in-flight data", "• This would boost Europe’s goal for scalable space return logistics, they say", "• Astrolight, a space communications startup, and ATMOS, a developer of reusable and modular return space capsules, are planning to run the test in 2027", "• This will be to enable real-time, resilient communication during re-entry", "• Basically, to support the return of in-orbit manufactured products"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in PCB ELECTRONICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in General concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and industry best practices.	pcb-electronics	General	["PCB ELECTRONICS", "General"]	14	https://www.electronicsweekly.com/news/business/astrolight-atmos-space-cargo-sign-mou-for-in-flight-laser-comms-2026-08/	Electronics Weekly	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80	\N	2026-08-13 09:35:03.765937+05:30	2026-08-13 09:35:03.76594+05:30	b02dfab9318cc274084d5b02cc9b5783ac524e626b3f034d60d4fd5e35e846b5	\N	processed	1	single_