Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck	curiously-minty-raspberry-pi-zero-cyberdeck	Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck\nThe Raspberry Pi community has been squeezing our hardware into Altoids tins since the beginning times (still not sure why), and the cyberdeck renaissance continues apace, so this Maker Monday, we thought we’d share a project by Excercising Ingenuity which marries the two.\nAltoids tins — they’ve been used for sewing kits, guitar effects pedals, and containers for the papers for hand-rolled cigarettes. They’re cheap, malleable, and thanks to the proliferation of US sweet shops adorning the UK’s high streets, they’re easily available even on Albion’s blessed shores.\nThis deceptively complicated build by Exercising Ingenuity features a tiny custom keyboard, a display, a Raspberry Pi Zero, and a 2-inch LCD display left over from another project. This screen didn’t work with the latest version of Raspberry Pi OS, so Mason (he of Exercising Ingenuity fame) had to drop back a couple of software versions and make some changes to the settings in raspi-config.\nAlso crammed into the tin is a UPS HAT and a battery for the Raspberry Pi Zero, plus a handmade protoboard dot-matrix keyboard, with a Waveshare RP2040 Zero to act as the keyboard’s microcontroller. Mason saved space by removing some unused components from the RP2040 Zero and Raspberry Pi Zero, and by soldering components directly together rather than using wires. He even found room for a USB hub so he could add an external USB port.\nHolding the electronics inside the tin required a spot of 3D printing — this took several design iterations, resulting in a mount printed out of a flexible filament that would bend to fit inside the tin (the rolled metal edges are slightly thicker than the rest of the material in the tin, so if you want an insert to fit flush, you can’t just slide it in from the top down).\nFinally, 15 GPIO pins are broken out, so you can use the device for a bit of hardware hacking. Labels for the ports and the GPIO pins are engraved into the tin, and in a nod to the olden days, the 3D-printed faceplate has been painted a lovely shade of beige.\nFind more projects like this in Raspberry Pi Official Magazine\nYou won’t find this build in the latest issue, however. It first featured in Raspberry Pi Official Magazine #167, which you can now read online for free.\nYou can also subscribe to the print version of our magazine. Not only do we deliver worldwide, but those who sign up to the six- or twelve-month print subscription will receive a FREE Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W!\nYou can find Raspberry Pi Official Magazine on Facebook, X, Threads, LinkedIn, and Mastodon. You can also contact the team via email: [email protected]	Marrying the Raspberry Pi community's enduring love for cyberdecks and building tiny projects inside Altoids tins	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:25.511763+05:30	\N	\N	116	200	2026-08-13 09:35:25.517896+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.26923+05:30	\N	1	https://www.raspberrypi.com/news/curiously-minty-raspberry-pi-zero-cyberdeck/	Raspberry Pi Blog	[Raspberry Pi Blog] Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck The Raspberry Pi community has been squeezing our hardware into Altoids tins since the beginning times (still not sure why), and the cyberdeck renaissance continues apace, so this Maker Mon...	### Event Overview\n[Raspberry Pi Blog] Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck Curiously minty Raspberry Pi Zero cyberdeck The Raspberry Pi community has been squeezing our hardware into Altoids tins since the beginning times (still not sure why), and the cyberdeck renaissance continues apace, so this Maker Monday, we thought we’d share a project by Excercising Ingenuity which marries the two Altoids tins — they’ve been used for sewing kits, guitar effects pedals, and containers for the papers for hand-rolled cigarettes They’re cheap, malleable, and thanks to the proliferation of US sweet shops adorning the UK’s high streets, they’re easily available even on Albion’s blessed shores.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nThis deceptively complicated build by Exercising Ingenuity features a tiny custom keyboard, a display, a Raspberry Pi Zero, and a 2-inch LCD display left over from another project This screen didn’t work with the latest version of Raspberry Pi OS, so Mason (he of Exercising Ingenuity fame) had to drop back a couple of software versions and make some changes to the settings in raspi-config Also crammed into the tin is a UPS HAT and a battery for the Raspberry Pi Zero, plus a handmade protoboard dot-matrix keyboard, with a Waveshare RP2040 Zero to act as the keyboard’s microcontroller.	["• Altoids tins — they’ve been used for sewing kits, guitar effects pedals, and containers for the papers for hand-rolled cigarettes", "• They’re cheap, malleable, and thanks to the proliferation of US sweet shops adorning the UK’s high streets, they’re easily available even on Albion’s blessed shores", "• This deceptively complicated build by Exercising Ingenuity features a tiny custom keyboard, a display, a Raspberry Pi Zero, and a 2-inch LCD display left over from another project", "• This screen didn’t work with the latest version of Raspberry Pi OS, so Mason (he of Exercising Ingenuity fame) had to drop back a couple of software versions and make some changes to the settings in raspi-config", "• Also crammed into the tin is a UPS HAT and a battery for the Raspberry Pi Zero, plus a handmade protoboard dot-matrix keyboard, with a Waveshare RP2040 Zero to act as the keyboard’s microcontroller"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in IOT directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in General concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and 