lash.com/photo-1558346490-a72e53ae2d4f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80	\N	2026-08-13 09:35:24.123591+05:30	2026-08-13 09:35:24.123592+05:30	113608da78df4605e76d51f13ca490b934a6c86879e4dc76b98c61cc77af8e01	\N	processed	1	single_source	[Arduino Blog] Meet Yadro Team: young robotics inventors from Uzbekistan, building tech that matters Meet Yadro Team: young robotics inventors from Uzbekistan, building tech that matters Sometimes, the most exciting projects we come across don’t come from well-funded labs or established companies...	[{"heading": "Event Overview", "paragraphs": ["[Arduino Blog] Meet Yadro Team: young robotics inventors from Uzbekistan, building tech that matters Meet Yadro Team: young robotics inventors from Uzbekistan, building tech that matters Sometimes, the most exciting projects we come across don’t come from well-funded labs or established companies – but rather from teenagers in a workshop, figuring things out because they genuinely want to solve something real That’s exactly the energy behind Yadro Team – an eight-member robotics group based in Uzbekistan, most of them under 20, founded in 2024 by Aziz Sayitkulov, who was all of 18 years old when he started it Aziz has been doing robotics for six years, and beyond leading the team he also teaches Arduino and robotics to other students."]}, {"heading": "Context & Technical Summary", "paragraphs": ["Because when you find something that changes the way you see the world, why not share it Before we get to their current project, it’s worth mentioning what first put Yadro Team on our radar: a pair of smart glasses for visually impaired people, built by team member Mukhammad Shukur The project made its way to Instagram and racked up millions of views."]}]	full	f	\N
Arduino Core on Zephyr 0.90.0 is officially stable and leaving beta	arduino-core-on-zephyr-0900-is-officially-stable-and-leaving-beta	Arduino Core on Zephyr 0.90.0 is officially stable and leaving beta\nWe are thrilled to announce the release of version 0.90.0 of the ArduinoCore-zephyr. This is a major milestone: we are officially exiting the beta phase and marking the core as stable. Achieving this goal is the culmination of months of testing, architectural refinement, and community feedback.\nAs we establish this core as our primary framework for advanced hardware, we are also beginning the deprecation phase for the corresponding legacy cores based on mbedOS, which has just now reached End of Life status.\nJoin us in this next chapter! Compile your existing sketches with version 0.90.0, and let us know how it performs.\nExpanded board and hardware support\nWelcome, Nano RP2040 Connect! The Arduino® Nano™ RP2040 Connect board officially joins our lineup. You can now leverage the full RTOS power of Zephyr on RP2040 hardware with standard Arduino API compatibility.\nSD card support for the Arduino® Portenta™ H7 and C33 boards. Native SD card support is now enabled for both Portenta H7 and Portenta C33, making it simple to log data and host local files on external memory.\nPDM library support. Official support for the PDM library on the Nano 33 BLE board is here, unlocking digital microphone input for audio sampling, noise detection, and edge AI voice processing.\nUniversal automatic loader sync\nWith version 0.90.0, automatic loader-and-sketch synchronization is now enabled across all supported boards. The core automatically verifies and handles loader updates behind the scenes during every sketch upload. This means you can simply open the Arduino® IDE, connect a board running a sketch based on mbedOS, switch to the new Zephyr-based core, and upload your sketch. The IDE will automatically update the loader as needed to ensure compatibility with Zephyr.\nIf you are running Zephyr for the first time, check out our step-by-step guide here.\nFull hardware compatibility list\n- Arduino® UNO™ Q board\n- Portenta H7\n- Portenta C33\n- Arduino® Opta™ microPLC\n- Arduino® GIGA R1™ WiFi board\n- Nano Matter board\n- Nano RP2040 Connect (New!)\n- Nano 33 BLE\n- Arduino® Nicla™ Sense ME module\n- Nicla Vision module\nHow to get started\nTo update your environment: open the Arduino IDE, search for “zephyr” in the Board Manager, and install version 0.90.0.\nThank you!\nThis stable milestone wouldn’t be possible without our community. Check out the full release notes on GitHub, report issues, and help us build the next era of Arduino on Zephyr!\nArduino, GIGA R1, Nano, Nicla, Opta, Portenta, and UNO are trademarks or registered trademarks of Arduino S.r.l.	We are thrilled to announce the release of version 0	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:24.106621+05:30	\N	\N	117	194	2026-08-13 09:35:24.120215+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.273567+05:30	\N	1	https://blog.arduino.cc/2026/07/29/arduino-core-on-zephyr-0-90-0-is-officially-stable-and-leaving-beta/	Arduino Blog	[Arduino Blog] Arduino Core on Zephyr 0. 0 is officially stable and leaving beta Arduino Core on Zephyr 0.	### Event Overview\n[Arduino Blog] Arduino Core on Zephyr 0 0 is officially stable and leaving beta Arduino Core on Zephyr 0 0 is officially stable and leaving beta We are thrilled to announce the release of version 0.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\n0 of the ArduinoCore-zephyr This is a major milestone: we are officially exiting the beta phase and marking the core as stable Achieving this goal is the culmination of months of testing, architectural refinement, and community feedback.	["• 0 is officially stable and leaving beta Arduino Core on Zephyr 0", "• 0 is officially stable and leaving beta We are thrilled to announce the release of version 0", "• 0 of the ArduinoCore-zephyr", "• This is a major milestone: we are officially exiting the beta phase and marking the core as stable", "• Ac