Webinar to discuss safety and scaling robot fleets in the warehouse	webinar-to-discuss-safety-and-scaling-robot-fleets-in-the-warehouse	Safety considerations can be a barrier to adoption and scaling of robot fleets in warehouses and other facilities, but they don’t have to be. Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction between compliance and operations teams.\nHowever, there are best practices for safely ensuring human-robot collaboration. A free webinar at 2:00 p.m. ET on Wednesday, Aug. 26, will discuss “When Robots and People Share the Floor: Safety That Keeps Fleets Moving.” Technology providers and warehousing experts will examine the challenges of planning, deploying, and managing robots alongside people in changing facilities and workflows.\nThe virtual panelists will explain how to address the following real-world issues:\n- How to handle risk assessments as fleets of automated guided vehicles (AGVs), autonomous mobile robots (AMRs), and autonomous forklifts grow\n- Working with automated storage and retrieval systems (ASRS) and other infrastructure\n- Where current safety practices can affect productivity\n- Lessons learned for smarter approaches to collision avoidance and associate safety\nRegister now to attend this webinar and get your questions answered during the live broadcast, as well as to watch afterwards on demand.\nAbout the warehouse safety experts\nJohn Calcio is vice president of partnerships at Redpoint Positioning, a Newton, Mass.–based manufacturer of ultra-wideband (UWB) industrial location and safety systems with more than 50,000 devices deployed worldwide.\nRedpoint’s flagship Crew Safety System is a SIL2/PLd-certified (IEC 61508, ISO 13849-1) functional safety solution for AGV and AMR OEMs. The company said it enables personnel to safely enter active robot fleets for interventions without triggering a full-fleet stop.\nCalcio leads Redpoint’s enterprise partnership strategy across the global robotics ecosystem, working with leading AMR/AGV manufacturers and material-handling integrators to embed certified functional safety into next-generation autonomous systems. He brings deep fluency in UWB/RTLS technology, functional safety certification, and robotics go-to-market dynamics. John is also a proponent for how certified safety layers can accelerate the safe deployment of collaborative human-robot operations at industrial scale.\nJohn Santagate is the founder and principal of EMP Advisors, an independent consulting firm helping retailers, manufacturers, distributors, and logistics organizations navigate warehouse automation, robotics, AI, and supply chain technology.\nWith nearly two decades in the field, Santagate has helped organizations evaluate emerging technologies, build automation strategies, and execute transformational initiatives. His career spans industry research, consulting, and executive leadership, including roles as an analyst at IDC and senior vice president of global robotics at Infios, where he led global robotics strategy and commercial growth.\nKnown for his vendor-independent perspective and practical approach, John advises executives on automation strategy, technology selection, and the evolving role of AI in supply chain operations. A TEDx speaker and frequent keynote presenter, he turns complex industry trends into clear, actionable insight.\nJonathan Cortellacci is a warehouse automation professional with extensive experience helping organizations optimize distribution operations through advanced robotics, intelligent automation, and supply chain innovation. At Geek+, he partners with customers to evaluate complex fulfillment challenges and develop strategies that use AMRs to increase productivity, improve operational flexibility, and deliver measurable business results.\nBefore joining Geek+, Cortellacci spent five years with Walmart, where he led efforts advancing one of the world’s most sophisticated supply chain networks. During his tenure, he helped lead large-scale automation initiatives spanning every major operational area within warehouse distribution centers, while also contributing to multiple next-generation fulfillment pilots and innovation programs. This hands-on experience with greenfield automation projects, brownfield retrofits, and emerging fulfillment technologies gave Cortellacci a unique perspective on the practical challenges of implementing automation at enterprise scale.\nA graduate of the University of Arkansas with a degree in industrial and systems engineering, Jonathan combines engineering expertise with a deep understanding of warehouse operations to design customer-centric solutions that deliver long-term value. His experience across retail, e-commerce, manufacturing, and third-party logistics enables him to help organizations build more agile, resilient, and future-ready supply chains.\nChris Morgan is senior director of research and development at Toyota Automated Logistics, where he leads high-performing, multidisciplinary teams advancing the next generation of robotics, artificial intelligence, firmware, software, and hardware solutions. With more than 28 years of technology leadership experience, Morgan is recognized for translating bold ideas into scalable innovations that transform material handling and logistics operations.\nBy combining deep technical expertise with strategic vision, Chris drives the development of intelligent, customer-centric solutions that address complex operational challenges, accelerate digital transformation, and shape the future of automation.\nEugene Demaitre, moderator, is the editorial director for robotics at Arrowfly (formerly WTWH Media), which produces Automated Warehouse, The Robot Report, RoboBusiness, and the Robotics Summit & Expo.\nPrior to working at Arrowfly, Demaitre was an editor at BNA (now part of Bloomberg), Computerworld, TechTarget (now part of Informa), and Robotics Business Review. He has participated in conferences worldwide, as well as spoken on numerous webcasts and podcasts.\nGene is always interested in learning more about robotics. He has a master’s from the George Washington University and lives in the Boston area.\nThis webinar is sponsored by Redpoint Positioning.	Industry experts will discuss how to scale mobile robots in the warehouse while maintaining both safety and productivity	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:19.147859+05:30	\N	\N	114	174	2026-08-13 09:35:19.166945+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.285821+05:30	\N	1	https://www.therobotreport.com/webinar-to-discuss-safety-scaling-robot-fleets-warehouse/	The Robot Report	[The Robot Report] Webinar to discuss safety and scaling robot fleets in the warehouse Safety considerations can be a barrier to adoption and scaling of robot fleets in warehouses and other facilities, but they don’t have to be. Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction betwee...	### Event Overview\n[The Robot Report] Webinar to discuss safety and scaling robot fleets in the warehouse Safety considerations can be a barrier to adoption and scaling of robot fleets in warehouses and other facilities, but they don’t have to be Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction between compliance and operations teams However, there are best practices for safely ensuring human-robot collaboration.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\nA free webinar at 2:00 p ET on Wednesday, Aug 26, will discuss “When Robots and People Share the Floor: Safety That Keeps Fleets Moving.	["• Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction between compliance and operations teams", "• However, there are best practices for safely ensuring human-robot collaboration", "• A free webinar at 2:00 p", "• ET on Wednesday, Aug", "• 26, will discuss “When Robots and People Share the Floor: Safety That Keeps Fleets Moving"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in ROBOTICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking general updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in General concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and industry best practices.	robotics	General	["ROBOTICS", "General"]	21	https://www.therobotreport.com/webinar-to-discuss-safety-scaling-robot-fleets-warehouse/	The Robot Report	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1546776310-eef45dd6d63c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80	\N	2026-08-13 09:35:19.166771+05:30	2026-08-13 09:35:19.166772+05:30	b0b96211e4edaa6b6e4ea5d130020ec79df3a34da44c71c127ac2a29489e8728	\N	processed	1	single_source	[The Robot Report] Webinar to discuss safety and scaling robot fleets in the warehouse Safety considerations can be a barrier to adoption and scaling of robot fleets in warehouses and other facilities, but they don’t have to be. Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction betwee...	[{"heading": "Event Overview", "paragraphs": ["[The Robot Report] Webinar to discuss safety and scaling robot fleets in the warehouse Safety considerations can be a barrier to adoption and scaling of robot fleets in warehouses and other facilities, but they don’t have to be Safety measures can lead to production stoppages and friction between compliance and operations teams However, there are best practices for safely ensuring human-robot collaboration."]}, {"heading": "Context & Technical Summary", "paragraphs": ["A free webinar at 2:00 p ET on Wednesday, Aug 26, will discuss “When Robots and People Share the Floor: Safety That Keeps Fleets Moving."]}]	full	f	\N
PlusAI reaches key milestones ahead of launching its autonomous trucks	plusai-reaches-key-milestones-ahead-of-launching-its-autonomous-trucks	PlusAI Inc. yesterday said it has updated its commercial readiness metrics for the first half of 2026. These metrics highlight the company’s progress towards deployments of factory-built autonomous trucks in 2027.\n“Every six months, we publish our commercial readiness metrics because they provide a transparent view into our progress,” said David Liu, co-founder and CEO of PlusAI. “The operational improvements combined with the commercial milestones our team has achieved recently show that PlusAI is making measurable progress toward deploying factory-built autonomous trucks at commercial scale.”\nFounded in 2016, PlusAI is developing an AI-based driver for autonomous trucks. The Santa Clara, Calif.-based company said it has continued to improve the safety validation, operational maturity, and efficiency of its SuperDrive software with global partners.\nAutonomous trucks advance in the first half of 2026\nDuring the first half this year, PlusAI said it has:\n- Launched a commercial autonomous fleet trial with International and Ryder on the I-35 freight corridor in Texas\n- Announced the first autonomous trucks in Southern Europe, in cooperation with Spanish logistics operator Sesé and the government of Aragon in Spain\n- Released SuperDrive 6.0, delivering AI and processing improvements as well as enabling night driving and construction-zone handling designed for scaled driverless operations\n- Expanded its collaboration with NVIDIA by using the Alpamayo foundation model, DRIVE Hyperion, and Halos safety technologies\n- Achieved four key ISO certifications supporting commercial deployment\n- Expanded international commercialization efforts through partnerships in Europe and Japan\nWhere does PlusAI stand on commercial readiness metrics?\nThrough the first half of 2026, PlusAI said its safety case readiness (SCR) has increased from 90.1% to 93.4%. The autonomous vehicle software developer also reported that its autonomous miles percentage (AMP) has grown from 99.2% to 99.8%, and its remote assistance-free trips (RAFT) have increased from 79% to 85.2% in the second half of 2025.\nSince introducing these metrics in 2025, PlusAI said it has steadily improved in all three indicators while expanding real-world operations. SCR measures progress toward completing the engineering evidence required to support safe commercial deployment.\nRAFT measures the percentage of trips completed without remote operator intervention, reflecting operational maturity and scalability. AMP measures the percentage of miles driven autonomously during commercial operations, explained the company.\nPlusAI said it expects to achieve 100% SCR in 2027 and more than 90% RAFT before commercial launch.\n“Commercializing autonomous trucks requires more than a capable AI system. It requires proving that the system is safe, reliable and operationally efficient while working closely with OEMs, fleets, and ecosystem partners,” stated Shawn Kerrigan, co-founder and chief operating officer of PlusAI. “Our progress across these metrics, together with the commercial milestones we’ve achieved this year, reflects the maturity of both our technology and our commercialization strategy.”	Since the start of the year, PlusAI said it has improved the safety validation, operational maturity, and efficiency of SuperDrive	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:19.147844+05:30	\N	\N	109	173	2026-08-13 09:35:19.165048+05:30	2026-08-14 11:19:13.286911+05:30	\N	1	https://www.therobotreport.com/plusai-reaches-key-milestones-ahead-launch-autonomous-trucks/	The Robot Report	[The Robot Report] PlusAI reaches key milestones ahead of launching its autonomous trucks PlusAI Inc. yesterday said it has updated its commercial readiness metrics for the first half of 2026.	### Event Overview\n[The Robot Report] PlusAI reaches key milestones ahead of launching its autonomous trucks PlusAI Inc yesterday said it has updated its commercial readiness metrics for the first half of 2026 These metrics highlight the company’s progress towards deployments of factory-built autonomous trucks in 2027.\n\n### Context & Technical Summary\n“Every six months, we publish our commercial readiness metrics because they provide a transparent view into our progress,” said David Liu, co-founder and CEO of PlusAI “The operational improvements combined with the commercial milestones our team has achieved recently show that PlusAI is making measurable progress toward deploying factory-built autonomous trucks at commercial scale ” Founded in 2016, PlusAI is developing an AI-based driver for autonomous trucks.	["• yesterday said it has updated its commercial readiness metrics for the first half of 2026", "• These metrics highlight the company’s progress towards deployments of factory-built autonomous trucks in 2027", "• “Every six months, we publish our commercial readiness metrics because they provide a transparent view into our progress,” said David Liu, co-founder and CEO of PlusAI", "• “The operational improvements combined with the commercial milestones our team has achieved recently show that PlusAI is making measurable progress toward deploying factory-built autonomous trucks at commercial scale", "• ” Founded in 2016, PlusAI is developing an AI-based driver for autonomous trucks"]	Detailed technical breakdown across 2 dynamic sections.	Advances in ROBOTICS directly impact system engineering workflows. Tracking autonomous robots updates enables engineers to optimize architectural decisions and mitigate operational risks.	For SIET engineering students: Reviewing this case study offers practical grounding in Autonomous Robots concepts, real-world deployment patterns, and industry best practices.	robotics	Autonomous Robots	["Autonomous"]	21	https://www.therobotreport.com/plusai-reaches-key-milestones-ahead-launch-autonomous-trucks/	The Robot Report	https://