Lockheed Martin demos AI sensor-driven autonomy on a fighter aircraft	lockheed-martin-demos-ai-sensor-driven-autonomy-on-a-fighter-aircraft	Lockheed Martin Skunk Works is highlighting that it has demonstrated sensor-driven autonomy on a fighter aircraft, albeit involving a simulation test aircraft.\nThe company’s advanced-projects division was working with the U.S. Air Force Test Pilot School (TPS).\nAn AI agent used targeting information from an operational sensor to execute air intercepts against a live target. Across eight flights, the X-62 Variable In-flight Simulation Test Aircraft (VISTA) executed 27 AI-controlled intercepts, states Lockheed.\nSensor-driven autonomy\n“Our ongoing partnership with TPS is driving important progress with this latest flight test series demonstrating that our AI can effectively and reliably close the sensor‑to‑action loop aboard an operational combat aircraft,” said Ron Fehlen, vp and general manager of Lockheed Martin Skunk Works.\n“Our autonomous agents consumed classified infrared search and track feeds and executed combat‑critical maneuvers in real time. This achievement marks a decisive advance toward delivering AI‑augmented air dominance for the United States.”\nThe X‑62 was equipped with the Lockheed Martin Legion Pod, tracking a live T‑38 jet. It feeds secure data to an AI agent that autonomously pilots the fighter into a tactical intercept position.\nYou can read more about the recent X-62 flight test activity on the Edwards Air Force Base website.\nThe next phase of the X-62 Mission Systems Upgrades (MSU) will allow the integration of the RTX PhantomStrike radar, and an expanded sensor and computing capacity.\nImage: Lockheed Martin\nSee also: Lockheed Martin unveils Skunk Works flexible factory	Lockheed Martin Skunk Works is highlighting that it has demonstrated sensor-driven autonomy on a fighter aircraft, albeit involving a simulation test aircraft	PUBLISHED	2026-08-13 09:35:03.750477+05:30	\N	\N	110	148	2026-08-13 09: